<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="cauchy-converge">
  <title>Cauchy Sequences are Convergent Sequences (and Vice-Versa)</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The following are some problems leading to the result that being convergent and being Cauchy are equivalent.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A,B \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> with <m>A \subseteq B</m>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>B</m> is bounded above, which is bigger, sup<m>(A)</m> or sup<m>(B)</m>? Prove.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>B</m> is bounded below, which is bigger, inf<m>(A)</m> or inf<m>(B)</m>? Prove.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\{ x_n \}</m> be a bounded sequence, let <m>S_n = \{ x_n, x_{n+1}, x_{n+2}, \ldots \}</m>.  Define <m>a_n =</m> inf<m>(S_n)</m> and <m>b_n =</m> sup<m>(S_n)</m>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ a_n \}</m> is an increasing sequence.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ b_n \}</m> is a decreasing sequence.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> are bounded.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\{ x_n \}</m> be a Cauchy sequence, and let <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> be as in #2.  Let <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ a_n \}</m> converges to some number <m>a</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ b_n \}</m> converges to some number <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>. Prove there exists <m>m \ge n</m> such that <m>\left| x_m - a_n \right|  &lt; \epsilon</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m>m \in \mathbb{N}</m>. Prove there exists <m>m \ge n</m> such that <m>\left| x_m - b_n \right|  &lt; \epsilon</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges to <m>a</m> or prove that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> converges to <m>b</m> (your choice). Hint: Use the facts that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> is Cauchy; that either <m>\{ a_n \}</m> converges to <m>a</m> or <m>\{ b_n \}</m> converges to <m>b</m>; either (c) or (d); and some triangle inequality magic.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>